 "action_summary": "Problem 2, taken literally: a similar task must be adaptable from one demonstration. The tree's own roadmap ranked the next operators by how often each refusal is asked. Priced instead of counted, that order was wrong: the 60-asking class offers ZERO reachable askings (the example itself cannot be read, so no apply-time operator exists), and the class with the biggest headline gain (+6.39 pp) loses precision 0.9241 -> 0.8038, adds 41 wrong changes against 14, and drives the per-question share to zero. Neither was shipped. The loss was somewhere else: the organ refuses a change wherever a call site has a shape the example did not show, because the new function may not take those arguments. That is right for a reshape and wrong for a rename between two names the standard library kept for each other -- assertEquals IS assertEqual, so it takes what it always took. That exemption is now in the organ, with a closed list of documented aliases. Measured on the shipped organ over the same 330 askings: strict coverage 0.2082 -> 0.2999, precision 0.9241 -> 0.9461, per-question share 0.2836 -> 0.6170, wrong changes 14 -> 14. This is a measured extension of an existing mechanism, not a new learner and not a sentience claim.",
